PART 7A: OWNER EMAIL VERIFICATION CODE
Goal: confirm the person signing in is the real owner of the account before granting owner status.
Flow:
1. The user signs in with Google (Part 6). The server checks the verified email against the server-side owner list.
2. If it matches an owner email, the server sends a 6-digit verification code to that same email address, from the sender "LinqStudiosLLC" (reuse the Part 8 email setup). The client is only told "verification required"; it never receives the owner list or the code. Nothing reveals to anyone else that an email is an owner email.
3. The app shows a "Verify it's you" screen. Until the code is confirmed, the account has NO owner powers.
4. When the correct code is entered, the server grants the owner claim (Part 7), the client refreshes its token, and the owner features unlock.
Code rules (all enforced on the server):
- 6 digits generated with a cryptographically secure random generator (for example crypto.randomInt), never Math.random.
- Stored only as a salted hash or HMAC, never in plain text. Compare with a constant-time comparison.
- Expires after 10 minutes. Single use. Bound to the user ID, the device ID, and the session that requested it. Requesting a new code invalidates the old one.
- Maximum 5 wrong attempts per code, then the code is dead and a new one must be requested. After 3 failed attempts, send me a security email: "Someone tried to verify your owner account."
- Resend: a 60-second cooldown, and at most 5 codes per hour per account. Also rate limit by IP.
- The code is sent ONLY to the email of the signed-in Google account, never to any other address, and never shown in the app, logs, or analytics.
Trusted devices and re-verification:
- After a successful verification, mark that device as verified on the server (store a hash of the device ID). The verified state stays with the permanent session from Part 6, so the code is not asked again on every app open.
- Ask for a new code when: the user signs in on a new device or browser, signs out and signs in again, the session is revoked, or 30 days have passed. Make the 30 days a single config value.
- Sign out removes the owner claim from that session and revokes its refresh tokens.
Verification screen UI:
- Title "Verify it's you", a short line, and the masked email (for example lj*****@gmail.com).
- 6 separate digit boxes with auto-focus, auto-advance, backspace support, paste support, and one-time-code autofill (autocomplete="one-time-code" on web). The numeric keyboard opens automatically.
- A countdown showing when the code expires, a "Resend code" button that is disabled during the 60-second cooldown, and a "Use a different account" button that signs out.
- Clear messages: wrong code (with attempts left), expired code, too many attempts (locked), no internet (with Retry), and email sent confirmation. Never reveal whether other accounts exist.
- Follows the active theme, touch targets at least 48x48, readable in light and dark mode, and works at 320 to 412px widths and in landscape.
- If the user closes the screen without verifying, they stay signed in as a normal public user with no owner features.
